# Resource: fivetran_connection_v2

~> This resource is in [Beta](https://fivetran.com/docs/core-concepts#releasephases).

Manages a Fivetran connection. Unlike `fivetran_connector`/`fivetran_connection`, `config` and `auth` are provided as dynamic, service-specific objects: their accepted fields, types, and required/readonly/immutable rules are resolved from connector metadata at plan time rather than from a fixed schema. This means the same resource works across services (e.g. `s3`, `postgres`, `fivetran_log`) without a per-service Terraform schema.

For detailed information about the required `config` and `auth` fields for each service type, see the [Fivetran API documentation](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/fivetran/fivetran/latest/docs/resources/connection_v2).

Pause state is managed separately via `fivetran_connection_v2_pause_state`; this resource does not expose a `paused` attribute. **New connections are always created paused.** To start syncing, add a linked `fivetran_connection_v2_pause_state` resource with `paused = false`.

## Example Usage

```hcl
resource "fivetran_connection_v2" "connection" {
    service  = "fivetran_log"
    group_id = "group_id"

    config = {
        schema = "my_schema"
    }
}

# The connection above is created paused. Unpause it to start syncing:
resource "fivetran_connection_v2_pause_state" "pause_state" {
    connection_id = fivetran_connection_v2.connection.id
    paused        = false
}
```

## Secrets in config

~> `config_secrets` and `config_secrets_version` are in [Public Preview](https://fivetran.com/docs/core-concepts#releasephases).

Fields set in `config` are stored in the Terraform state. To keep secret fields such as passwords out of the plan and state, set them in the write-only `config_secrets` attribute instead. It accepts [ephemeral values](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/resources/ephemeral), for example from a secrets manager, and requires Terraform 1.11 or later.

```hcl
ephemeral "google_secret_manager_secret_version" "db_password" {
    secret = "fivetran-db-password"
}

resource "fivetran_connection_v2" "connection" {
    service                  = "postgres"
    group_id                 = "group_id"
    destination_schema_names = "FIVETRAN_NAMING"

    config = {
        host = "db.example.com"
        port = 5432
        user = "fivetran"
    }

    config_secrets = {
        password = ephemeral.google_secret_manager_secret_version.db_password.secret_data
    }
    config_secrets_version = 1
}
```

- The `config_secrets` fields are merged with `config` when the connection is created or updated. Setting the same field in both is an error.
- Terraform doesn't store `config_secrets`, so it can't detect that a value changed. To send new values, for example after rotating a password, change `config_secrets_version`.
- To move a field from `config` to `config_secrets`, remove it from `config` and add it to `config_secrets`. The next apply removes it from state and sends mutable values to the API, without changing `config_secrets_version`.
- Immutable and read-only fields are excluded from update PATCH requests, including during migration and later version changes. Moving an unchanged immutable field only removes its stored value. Changing an immutable field during migration requires replacing the connection. After migration, changing its write-only value cannot be detected; changing `config_secrets_version` does not update immutable fields.
- After `terraform import`, secret fields are stored as the masked value the API returns (`******`), so Terraform can't verify them. Setting the real value of an immutable secret, in `config` or by moving it to `config_secrets`, updates the connection in place instead of replacing it, with a warning. A different value isn't sent to the API.
- To manage the secrets of an imported connection, set them in `config_secrets` together with `config_secrets_version`. The imported state has no version, so the first apply sends the mutable values, and the secrets are never stored in state. A secret left in `config` is imported as the masked value, so the first plan updates the connection in place to send it. Later plans compare it with the stored value.
- If all fields move to `config_secrets`, you can omit `config` entirely. The next apply removes the migrated fields from the current state. Previous state snapshots and backups are not modified.
- Removing a field from `config_secrets` doesn't clear it in Fivetran.

## Secrets in auth

~> `auth_secrets` and `auth_secrets_version` are in [Public Preview](https://fivetran.com/docs/core-concepts#releasephases).

Fields set in `auth` are stored in the Terraform state. To keep secret fields such as client secrets and refresh tokens out of the plan and state, set them in the write-only `auth_secrets` attribute instead. It works like `config_secrets`, accepts [ephemeral values](https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/resources/ephemeral), and requires Terraform 1.11 or later. The accepted fields depend on the service.

```hcl
ephemeral "google_secret_manager_secret_version" "client_secret" {
    secret = "fivetran-client-secret"
}

ephemeral "google_secret_manager_secret_version" "refresh_token" {
    secret = "fivetran-refresh-token"
}

resource "fivetran_connection_v2" "connection" {
    service  = "service_name"
    group_id = "group_id"

    config = {
        schema = "my_schema"
    }

    auth_secrets = {
        client_access = {
            client_id     = "my_client_id"
            client_secret = ephemeral.google_secret_manager_secret_version.client_secret.secret_data
        }
        refresh_token = ephemeral.google_secret_manager_secret_version.refresh_token.secret_data
    }
    auth_secrets_version = 1
}
```

- The `auth_secrets` fields are merged with `auth` when the connection is created or updated. Setting the same top-level field in both is an error, so a nested object such as `client_access` must be set entirely in one of them.
- Terraform doesn't store `auth_secrets`, so it can't detect that a value changed. To send new values, for example after rotating a token, change `auth_secrets_version`.
- To move a field from `auth` to `auth_secrets`, remove it from `auth` and add it to `auth_secrets`. The next apply removes it from state and sends mutable values to the API, without changing `auth_secrets_version`.
- Immutable and read-only fields are excluded from update PATCH requests, including during migration and later version changes. Moving an unchanged immutable field only removes its stored value. Changing an immutable field during migration requires replacing the connection. After migration, changing its write-only value cannot be detected; changing `auth_secrets_version` does not update immutable fields.
- The API never returns `auth`, so after `terraform import` neither `auth` nor `auth_secrets` is known to Terraform. Set `auth_secrets_version` together with `auth_secrets`: the first apply sends the mutable values because the version changes from unset.
- Removing a field from `auth_secrets` doesn't clear it in Fivetran.

